narrative:
## Refactoring Into Clean Modules

Good module organization follows three rules:
1. **One responsibility per module**
2. **Dependencies flow in one direction**
3. **Public API is minimal**

### Before: Messy Organization

```xiom
module app {
  pub type User = { name: Str; age: Int; }

  pub fn validate_age(age: Int) -> Bool { return age > 0 & age < 150; }

  pub fn display_user(user: &User) {
    io.println(user.name + ", " + string.int_to_str(user.age));
  }

  pub fn calculate_discount(age: Int) -> Int {
    if age > 65 { return 20; }
    if age < 18 { return 10; }
    return 0;
  }

  pub fn format_email(user: &User) -> Str {
    return user.name + "@company.com";
  }
}
```

Everything in one module — data, validation, display, business logic, formatting. Hard to find things, hard to test, hard to change.

### After: Clean Organization

```xiom
module models {
  pub type User = { name: Str; age: Int; }
}

module validation {
  pub fn is_valid_age(age: Int) -> Bool {
    return age > 0 & age < 150;
  }
  pub fn is_senior(age: Int) -> Bool { return age >= 65; }
  pub fn is_minor(age: Int) -> Bool { return age < 18; }
}

module discounts {
  pub fn for_user(age: Int) -> Int {
    if validation.is_senior(age) { return 20; }
    if validation.is_minor(age)  { return 10; }
    return 0;
  }
}

module display {
  pub fn user_profile(user: &models.User) {
    io.println(user.name + ", age " + string.int_to_str(user.age));
  }
}

module email {
  pub fn build_address(user: &models.User) -> Str {
    return user.name + "@company.com";
  }
}
```

### The Refactoring Checklist

1. **Extract data types** into a `models` module (no logic, just types)
2. **Extract validation** into a `validation` module (pure functions, no side effects)
3. **Extract business logic** into domain modules (`discounts`, `pricing`, `scoring`)
4. **Extract display/IO** into presentation modules (`display`, `formatting`)
5. **Keep the main module thin** — it should only wire modules together

### The Dependency Rule

```
models  <--  validation  <--  discounts  <--  display
  ^            ^                  ^              ^
  |            |                  |              |
  +--- leaf modules: don't depend on anything
```

Higher-level modules can use lower-level modules. Never the reverse. `models` should never import `display` — that would be a data layer depending on a presentation layer, which makes both harder to change.

